From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS stays Unix-side, like the toolkits' variables (5951)

ntdll: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS stays Unix-side, like the toolkits' variables

The home folder is the Windows profile too, and Java reads
.accessibility.properties there. A Windows Java with the Access Bridge
turned on (NVDA's setup does it) names com.sun.java.accessibility.AccessBridge
in it, and every Linux Java program then failed to start: "AWTError:
Assistive Technology not found" (Arduino IDE, the SG Store walk on qa5).
The session tells Linux programs to load none
(sg-common.sh SG_LINUX_APP_ENV: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS=
-Djavax.accessibility.assistive_technologies=). That must not reach a
Windows Java, which would lose its Access Bridge: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS is now
Unix-only like GDK_ and SDL_ (5946): hidden from the Windows environment,
passed on to the Unix programs a Windows one starts. A value the Windows
side sets itself still reaches its Windows children.
Gate: test/toolkitenv-gate.sh (JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS checks); mutant
SG_MUTANT_JAVA_ENV_VISIBLE.
---
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/env.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/env.c
@@ -336,6 +336,19 @@
  * Check if an environment variable needs to be handled specially when
  * passed through the Unix environment (i.e. prefixed with "WINE").
  */
+/* Stained Glass OS: Java's options for the session's Linux programs (sg-common.sh, SG_LINUX_APP_ENV:
+ * no assistive technology from the home folder's .accessibility.properties, which the Windows profile
+ * shares; a Windows Java, or NVDA's setup, names the Access Bridge there and every Linux Java program
+ * then failed to start: "Assistive Technology not found", Arduino IDE). They stay Unix-side with the
+ * toolkits' below: a Windows Java keeps the Access Bridge it is told to load. */
+static BOOL is_java_env_var( const char *var )
+{
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_JAVA_ENV_VISIBLE
+    return FALSE;
+#endif
+    return STARTS_WITH( var, "JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS=" );
+}
+
 /* Stained Glass OS: the session tells Linux programs to draw on X11 (sg-common.sh, SG_LINUX_APP_ENV:
  * GDK_BACKEND=x11, SDL_VIDEODRIVER=x11, ...). A Windows build of the same toolkit reads them too and
  * has no X11: Geany (GTK) said "cannot open display" and ended at once when started from Start or the
@@ -350,7 +363,8 @@
     return (STARTS_WITH( var, "GDK_" ) ||
             STARTS_WITH( var, "GTK_" ) ||
             STARTS_WITH( var, "GSK_" ) ||
-            STARTS_WITH( var, "SDL_" ));
+            STARTS_WITH( var, "SDL_" ) ||
+            is_java_env_var( var ));
 }
 
 static BOOL is_special_env_var( const char *var )
